t0\tattest/records/158/spec-01.json\n29\t0\tattest/records/158/spec-02.json\n13\t0\tattest/records/158/spec-03.json\n13\t0\tattest/records/158/spec-04.json\n34\t0\tattest/records/158/spec-05.json\n44\t0\tattest/records/158/spec-06.json\n13\t0\tattest/records/158/spec-07.json\n83\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-01.json\n19\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-01.log\n1\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-02.filed\n40\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-02.json\n7\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-02.started\n1\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-03.filed\n40\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-03.json\n7\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-03.started\n1\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-04.filed\n40\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-04.json\n7\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-04.started\n7\t0\tattest/records/158/verify-05.started\n48\t0\tattest/specs/158/criteria/doctor-fails-on-an-unsealed-merge.sh\n18\t0\tattest/specs/158/criteria/doctor-fails-without-attestation-command.sh\n56\t0\tattest/specs/158/criteria/doctor-names-an-unmerged-item.sh\n35\t0\tattest/specs/158/criteria/doctor-says-not-read-when-the-store-cannot-be-read.sh\n15\t0\tattest/specs/158/criteria/the-doctor-job-carries-the-token.sh\n2\t0\tattest/specs/158/criteria/the-proof-has-its-test.sh\n28\t0\tattest/specs/158/spec.md\n8\t0\tattest/specs/158/stub/.attavo/bin/forge-attestation\n101\t0\tattest/specs/158/stub/.github/workflows/ci.yml\n53\t0\tattest/specs/158/stub/attavo.toml\n3926\t0\tattest/specs/158/stub/bin/attavo\n262\t0\tattest/specs/158/stub/schema/manifest.schema.json\n141\t0\tattest/specs/158/stub/tests/test_doctor_sealed.py\n102\t2\tbin/attavo\n10\t0\tschema/manifest.schema.json\n11\t2\ttests/test_doctor.py\n169\t0\ttests/test_doctor_sealed.py\n6\t5\ttests/test_record_schemas.py\n    both from the adapter's own report line (derive_stop's report_class)\n    now instead.\"\"\"\n    text = text or \"\"\n    for name in STOP_CLASSES:\n        markers = CLASS_TEXT_MARKERS.get(name, (name,))\n        if any(marker in text for marker in markers):\n            return name\n    return None\n\n\ndef derive_stop(status: str, *, refusal: str | None = None, halted: dict | None = None,\n                log: str | None = None, report_class: str | None = None,\n                refusal_provenance: str | None = None) -> dict | None:\n    \"\"\"The stop field for a build or verify record: None for status \"pass\",\n    else {class, provenance, detail} from what the caller already knows.\n\n      - report_class -- payload.get(\"class\") off the adapter's own report\n        line (issue #88, #94), when it names a STOP_CLASSES member -- is\n        that class, provenance \"executor\". build.schema.json and\n        verify.schema.json stay untouched by issue #88 on purpose, so the\n        class lives only on the report line, never a structured field\n        cmd_build or cmd_verify forwards on its own account; the caller\n        reads payload itself and passes this in, since only it still has\n        payload once an iteration ends. This is non-question's and\n        outlived-invocation's only path to a record now (issue #94):\n        CLASS_TEXT_MARKERS disables both for the text scan below, the\n        same way issue #93 disabled ceiling's bare-name match there. A\n        class an adapter does not yet put on its report line --\n        outlived-invocation today, since that refusal path prints no\n        report line at all -- falls to unclassed below, same as any\n        other unnamed cause, until the adapter that raises it puts a\n        class on the line the way non-question already does.\n      - a halt carrying a non-empty halted.question, once it is not a\n        non-question caught above, is a genuine question -- question/spec,\n        with the question's own first line as detail (issue #100's\n        followups). This is the specimen issue #100 itself was built to\n        retire: tenant zero's #663 build-01, a proper question, classed\n        unclassed/product and filed as a product defect with the wrong\n        provenance -- a question is about the definition of done, which is\n        a person's or a ruling's to answer, never the product's own\n        mechanism.\n      - failing that, a refusal or the log itself that already names its\n        class in text (issue #88, #93 for ceiling's own pre-emptive\n        refusal) is that class, provenance \"executor\" -- the log is\n        checked because neither adapter's refusal *field* carries the\n        class today; the class-naming line each adapter prints reaches\n        only the transcript\n      - a refusal that is not that -- the write-outside-claims path\n        itself, not the \"commit ... not authored by the executor\" shape\n        -- is claims-boundary/spec, unless the caller already knows this\n        refusal touched paths.records or paths.specs (issue #140:\n        protected_path_touched, checked ahead of the ordinary claims\n        match in cmd_build) and passes refusal_provenance=\"executor\" --\n        the product's own directories are not a claim a spec makes, so a\n        refusal there is not the spec's boundary to answer for\n      - a returned verify (the forge was not green; no adapter ran) is\n        forge-unconcluded/tenant\n      - an adapter exit whose log carries a declared-ceiling error line is\n        ceiling/product\n      - anything else is unclassed/product, with the log's last line\n        (capped at UNCLASSED_DETAIL_MAX_CHARS) as its detail -- the other\n        class that carries a detail, since every remaining class's\n        evidence already lives in refusal, halted, forge_checks, or the\n        log itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    if status == \"pass\":\n        return None\n    if status == \"returned\":\n        return {\"class\": \"forge-unconcluded\", \"provenance\": \"tenant\", \"detail\": None}\n    if report_class in STOP_CLASSES:\n        return {\"class\": report_class, \"provenance\": \"executor\", \"detail\": None}\n    question = (halted or {}).get(\"question\")\n    if status == \"halted\" and question:\n        return {\"class\": \"question\", \"provenance\": \"spec\",\n               \"detail\": question.splitlines()[0]}\n    known = _carries_a_known_class(refusal) or _carries_a_known_class(log)\n    if known:\n        return {\"class\": known, \"provenance\": \"executor\", \"detail\": None}\n    if status == \"refused\" and refusal is not None and not refusal.startswith(\"commit \"):\n        return {\"class\": \"claims-boundary\", \"provenance\": refusal_provenance or \"spec\",\n               \"detail\": None}\n    if log and any(marker in log.lower() for marker in CEILING_MARKERS):\n        return {\"class\": \"ceiling\", \"provenance\": \"product\", \"detail\": None}\n    return {\"class\": \"unclassed\", \"provenance\": \"product\", \"detail\": _log_last_line(log)}\n\n\ndef gate_stop(refusal: str | None) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"The stop for a refused spec-gate record: class \"gate\", provenance\n    \"spec\", the refusal string itself as cause (issue #137) -- not\n    derive_stop's generic claims-boundary/spec fallback issue #133\n    reused, which named a boundary the record never crossed: \"criterion\n    \"\"\"The executor's identity (issue #33): set by the product, never the\n    tenant's git config, so an executor commit is never indistinguishable\n    from an operator's own -- the specimen is attavo/code-factory@e22fa85.\n    A reserved, undeliverable domain (.invalid) so no mailbox is implied.\n    Distinct from the records app identity: the app says the product\n    pushed this, this says an invocation authored this -- different\n    facts, neither borrows the other's name.\"\"\"\n    return {\"name\": f\"attavo executor ({adapter_name})\",\n            \"email\": f\"executor+{adapter_name}@attavo.invalid\"}\n\n\ndef driver_identity() -> dict:\n    \"\"\"The driver's identity: set by the product, never the tenant's git\n    config, so a driver's own transport commit -- a record commit\n    `commit_record` makes on the driver's behalf -- is never\n    indistinguishable from an operator's own; `git log --author='attavo\n    driver'` on a driven item lists exactly those commits. Distinct from\n    executor_identity: that names an adapter invocation's own commits,\n    this names the transport that carries a stage's record.\"\"\"\n    return {\"name\": \"attavo driver\", \"email\": \"driver@attavo.invalid\"}\n\n\n# The stop classes issue #89 gave no code of their own beyond the guard\n# that raised them -- non-question and outlived-invocation from an\n# adapter's own pre-emptive refusal, forge-unconcluded from a returned\n# verify, ceiling from a declared-input-limit error, claims-boundary from\n# the write-outside-claims refusal, repeated-act from... the same shape,\n# environment from cmd_build's own [checks].command preflight (issue\n# #115). question (a person's to answer) and the residual (no-cause,\n# unclassed -- nobody has looked yet) are not in this tuple; attavo stops\n# (issue #129) is the one reader, so it lives beside driver_identity,\n# not beside derive_stop, which never needs to tell a guard from any\n# other class once it has already named one.\nGUARDED_STOP_CLASSES = (\"non-question\", \"outlived-invocation\", \"forge-unconcluded\", \"ceiling\",\n                        \"claims-boundary\", \"repeated-act\", \"environment\")\n\n\ndef stop_class_enum() -> tuple[str, ...]:\n    \"\"\"Every class a stop can name, read from the build record schema's own\n    enum (issue #129) rather than retyped -- STOP_CLASSES already drifted\n    once (environment arrived in the schema before anything but derive_stop\n    read it); a second copy is a second place to forget. build's schema is\n    read, not verify's: verify's own enum is a strict subset (it never\n    raises environment, cmd_build's own preflight alone).\"\"\"\n    schema = stage_schema(\"build\")\n    return tuple(schema[\"properties\"][\"stop\"][\"properties\"][\"class\"][\"enum\"])\n\n\ndef commits_since(root: Path, start_sha: str, end: str = \"HEAD\") -> list[dict]:\n    \"\"\"(sha, name, email) for every commit after start_sha up to and\n    including end, first-parent only, oldest first. cmd_build's own call\n        \"stop\": derive_stop(status_out, refusal=refusal, halted=halted,\n                            log=\"\\n\".join(transcripts),\n                            report_class=(payload or {}).get(\"class\"),\n                            refusal_provenance=refusal_provenance),\n    }\n    record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, \"build\", record, m, item_id,\n                               log=\"\\n\".join(transcripts))\n\n    print(f\"attavo build: {status_out} ({record_path.name})\")\n    if status_out == \"halted\":\n        return BUILD_HALT_EXIT\n    return 0 if status_out == \"pass\" else 1\n\n\n# --- verify: the forge's own read first, a judge only once it is green ---\n\nFORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS = {\"success\", \"neutral\", \"skipped\"}\n# issue #84: a concluded check's second token is a conclusion --\n# FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS, or another concluded one such as failure,\n# cancelled, or timed_out; an unconcluded check's is a status instead,\n# one of these. drive's own poll (poll_forge_checks) is the one reader\n# that needs to tell a still-running check apart from a concluded, not-ok\n# one -- read_forge_checks itself only ever needed \"green or not\".\nFORGE_UNCONCLUDED_STATUSES = {\"queued\", \"in_progress\", \"pending\", \"waiting\"}\n\n\ndef read_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> dict:\n    \"\"\"Runs the tenant's [forge].checks_command on sha; the product does\n    not know what forge that command reads. One line per check as\n    \"<name> <conclusion>\"; a command that fails to run, or any conclusion\n    other than success/neutral/skipped, is not green.\"\"\"\n    run = subprocess.run(f\"{checks_command} {shlex.quote(sha)}\", shell=True, cwd=str(root),\n                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n    conclusions = []\n    for line in run.stdout.splitlines():\n        line = line.strip()\n        if not line:\n            continue\n        parts = line.rsplit(\" \", 1)\n        if len(parts) == 2:\n            conclusions.append({\"name\": parts[0], \"conclusion\": parts[1]})\n    # A commit the forge has no record of is not green -- it is unread.\n    # The executor's own commit is exactly this: the forge attaches\n    # checks to a pull request's head, and that commit stops being the\n    # head the moment a record commit lands on top of it. all() over an\n    # empty list is True, which is why this is spelled out rather than\n    # implied by the loop above.\n    ok = run.returncode == 0 and bool(conclusions) and all(\n        c[\"conclusion\"] in FORGE_OK_CONCLUSIONS for c in conclusions)\n    return {\"command\": checks_command, \"sha\": sha, \"conclusions\": conclusions}, ok\n\n\ndef diff_since_approval(root: Path, approval_sha: str | None) -> tuple[str, str | None]:\n    \"\"\"(diff_text, refusal): the item's own work since approval, not\n    everything merged onto the branch since then (issue #52) -- a\n    three-dot diff against approval_sha itself is not enough, since\n    approval_sha is already an ancestor of HEAD in the normal flow and\n    merge-base(approval_sha, HEAD) is just approval_sha again (verified\n    empirically, see issue #52). Diffing from the merge-base of HEAD and\n    the remote's default branch instead excludes content that arrived by\n    merging that branch in, because that content is already present on\n    both sides of the merge base. refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is what names\n    the default branch without hard-coding \"main\"; when it is not set,\n    there is nothing to diff against and this refuses rather than guess.\n    (\"\", None) when approval_sha is falsy -- nothing to diff from at all.\n    Shared by cmd_verify and cmd_counsel, which both hand an adapter this\n    same diff.\"\"\"\n    if not approval_sha:\n        return \"\", None\n    symref = subprocess.run(\n        [\"git\", \"-C\", str(root), \"symbolic-ref\", \"-q\", \"refs/remotes/origin/HEAD\"],\n        capture_output=True, text=True, check=False)\n    if symref.returncode != 0:\n        return \"\", \"refs/remotes/origin/HEAD is not set; run `git remote set-head origin -a`\"\n    diff_base = git(root, \"merge-base\", symref.stdout.strip(), \"HEAD\").strip()\n    return git(root, \"diff\", diff_base, \"HEAD\"), None\n\n\ndef cmd_verify(argv: list[str]) -> int:\n    root = Path(\".\").resolve()\n    item_id = argv[0] if argv else derive_item_id(root)\n    if not item_id:\n        print(\"attavo verify: cannot derive item id; pass N or run on an attavo/N-* branch\",\n              file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n    m = read_manifest(root)\n    if m is None:\n        print(f\"attavo verify: no {MANIFEST} at {root}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2\n\n    specs_rel = m[\"paths\"][\"specs\"]\n    records_rel = m[\"paths\"][\"records\"]\n    item_records_dir = root / records_rel / item_id\n\n    latest_build = latest_record(item_records_dir, \"build\")\n    if latest_build is None:\n        print(f\"attavo verify: refused: no build record for item {item_id}\")\n        return 1\n    if latest_build.get(\"status\") != \"pass\":\n        print(f\"attavo verify: refused: latest build record is \"\n              f\"{latest_build.get('status')!r}, not pass\")\n        return 1\n\n    forge_cfg = m.get(\"forge\") or {}\n    checks_command = forge_cfg.get(\"checks_command\")\n    if not checks_command:\n        print(\"attavo verify: refused: no [forge].checks_command in the manifest\")\n        return 1\n\n    build_sha = latest_build[\"sha\"]\n    build_record_path = latest_attempt_path(item_records_dir, \"build\")\n    started = time.monotonic()\n    head_sha = git(root, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\").strip()\n    # The forge attaches checks to a pull request's head, not to the\n    # executor's own commit -- that commit stops being the head the\n    # moment a record commit lands on top of it (issue #44). Reading the\n    # forge on head_sha is what the forge actually checked and what will\n    # be merged; build_sha and forge_checks[\"sha\"] can differ, and both\n    # are kept on the record rather than one sha serving two purposes.\n    # head_sha is a marker commit just pushed (issue #178): the forge has\n    # not attached anything to it yet on the first read, so this polls\n    # with the same bound and interval the drive's own guard uses, giving\n    # those checks room to conclude before this read judges them.\n    forge_checks, forge_ok = poll_forge_checks(root, checks_command, head_sha)\n\n    if not forge_ok:\n        record = {\n            \"sha\": head_sha,\n            \"build_sha\": build_sha,\n            \"build_record\": build_record_path.stem if build_record_path else None,\n            \"approval_sha\": latest_build.get(\"approval_sha\"),\n            \"at\": now_iso(),\n            \"adapter\": None, \"adapter_config\": {}, \"adapter_version\": None,\n            \"model\": None, \"model_version\": None,\n            \"reports\": [],\n            \"forge_checks\": forge_checks,\n            \"findings\": None,\n            \"tests_named\": [],\n            \"commits\": [],\n            \"mutation\": None, \"weak_stub\": False, \"stub_ratio\": None,\n            \"tokens\": {\"input\": 0, \"output\": 0}, \"cost_usd\": None,\n            \"wall_clock_s\": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),\n            \"status\": \"returned\",\n            \"stop\": derive_stop(\"returned\"),\n        }\n        record_path = write_record(item_records_dir, \"verify\", record, m, item_id)\n        print(f\"attavo verify: returned ({record_path.name}); the forge checks are not green\")\n        return 0\n\n    executor = m.get(\"executor\") or {}\n    adapter_name = executor.get(\"verify\") or executor.get(\"build\")\n    if not adapter_name:\n        print(\"attavo verify: refused: no [executor].verify or [executor].build in the manifest\")\n        return 1\n    adapter_path = resolve_adapter(root, adapter_name)\n    if adapter_path is None:\n        print(f\"attavo verify: refused: no adapter named {adapter_name!r}\")\n        return 1\n\n    adapter_config = merge_adapter_config(m, adapter_name, \"verify\")\n    reports = adapter_reports(adapter_path)\n        \"reports\": reports,\n        \"tokens\": tokens,\n        \"cost_usd\": cost_usd,\n        \"wall_clock_s\": round(time.monotonic() - started, 3),\n        \"status\": status_out,\n        \"class\": cls,\n        \"verdict\": verdict,\n        \"citations\": citations,\n        \"proposal\": proposal,\n    }\n    record_out_path = write_record(item_records_dir, \"counsel\", record, m, item_id, log=transcript)\n\n    print(f\"attavo counsel: {status_out} ({record_out_path.name})\")\n    return 0 if status_out == \"done\" else 1\n\n\n# --- drive: the mechanical transitions between approval and ready-to-merge\n# (issue #84) -- one step per invocation, and only that step, until a\n# state that belongs to a person. Writes no record of its own; the stage\n# records already on disk are the only state it reads. ---\n\ndef forge_checks_unconcluded(forge_checks: dict) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Whether poll_forge_checks should keep waiting rather than stop at\n    once: an empty read (the forge has not attached any check to this sha\n    yet) or any one check still carrying a status (queued, in_progress,\n    pending, waiting) rather than a concluded conclusion. A concluded\n    conclusion that is not green (failure, cancelled, timed_out, and\n    anything else read_forge_checks did not recognize as ok) is not this\n    -- that check is done, whether or not it passed.\"\"\"\n    conclusions = forge_checks[\"conclusions\"]\n    if not conclusions:\n        return True\n    return any(c[\"conclusion\"] in FORGE_UNCONCLUDED_STATUSES for c in conclusions)\n\n\ndef poll_forge_checks(root: Path, checks_command: str, sha: str) -> tuple[dict, bool]:\n    \"\"\"read_forge_checks, retried within drive_forge_poll_bound_s() while\n    some check is still unconcluded (forge_checks_unconcluded) -- a check\n    still running is not yet red, and drive gives it a little room to\n    conclude before it gives up. A read where every check has already\n    concluded, green or not, stops here at once: there is nothing left to\n    wait on. The bound and interval are constants this lane names, not a\n    manifest key, re-read at call time so a test's own clock\n    (ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_BOUND_S, ATTAVO_DRIVE_POLL_INTERVAL_S) never has to\n    survive past when it was set.\"\"\"\n    bound = drive_forge_poll_bound_s()\n    interval = drive_forge_poll_interval_s()\n    deadline = time.monotonic() + bound\n    forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)\n    while not ok and forge_checks_unconcluded(forge_checks) and time.monotonic() < deadline:\n        time.sleep(interval)\n        forge_checks, ok = read_forge_checks(root, checks_command, sha)\n    return forge_checks, ok\n\n\ndef commit_record(root: Path, record_paths: Path | list[Path], message: str) -> None:\n    \"\"\"`git commit --only` on every record path given, and each one's .log\n    and .filed, each if one exists -- never the whole index. A transport\n    commit that sweeps the index is exactly the regression issue #46\n    already guards build and verify against on the adapter side; drive\n    must not reintroduce it on the transport side. The .filed marker\n    (issue #89) is committed alongside the record it names, in the same\n    commit and the same push, so a second worktree or a fresh clone reads\n    the same filed fact rather than refiling every stop it finds with no\n    marker of its own. A single Path is accepted for the common one-record\n    case; issue #100 passes a list so the counsel record lands in the same\n    commit as the stage record it counsels. Authored and committed as\n    driver_identity(), set by the product and never the tenant's git\n    config, so a driven item's record commits are never indistinguishable\n    from an operator's own.\"\"\"\n    if isinstance(record_paths, Path):\n        record_paths = [record_paths]\n    paths: list[str] = []\n    for record_path in record_paths:\n        paths.append(str(record_path.relative_to(root)))\n        log_path = record_path.with_suffix(\".log\")\n\n    message = f\"attavo: {stage} record for item {item_id} ({after.stem})\"\n    try:\n        commit_record(root, to_commit, message)\n    except RuntimeError as exc:\n        print(f\"attavo drive: stopped: could not commit the {stage} record: {exc}\")\n        return 1\n\n    ok, err = push_record_commit(root, branch)\n    if not ok:\n        print(f\"attavo drive: stopped: could not push the {stage} record: {err}\")\n        return 1\n\n    if status_out == \"halted\":\n        question = (record.get(\"halted\") or {}).get(\"question\")\n        print(f\"attavo drive: stopped: {stage} halted: {question} ({after.name}); \"\n              f\"pushed to {branch}\")\n    elif status_out != \"pass\":\n        reason = record.get(\"refusal\") or status_out\n        print(f\"attavo drive: stopped: {stage} {status_out}: {reason} ({after.name}); \"\n              f\"pushed to {branch}\")\n    else:\n        print(f\"attavo drive: pushed {after.name} to {branch}\")\n    return rc, status_out != \"pass\"\n\n\ndef _drive_step(root: Path, m: dict, item_id: str) -> tuple[int, bool]:\n    \"\"\"One mechanical step, exactly as a single `attavo drive` call always\n    has -- and whether repetition under --until-ready should stop here:\n    True for every refusal or stop (no valid approval, no\n    [forge].checks_command, the forge not green, or a stage that wrote a\n    non-pass record) and for the ready-to-merge terminal state; False\n    only when a stage just passed, so drive_state may have a further\n    stage to pursue on the next call.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        stage, extra, records_dir = drive_state(root, m, item_id)\n    except ShallowCheckoutError as exc:\n        print(f\"attavo drive: refused: {exc}\", file=sys.stderr)\n        return 2, True\n\n    if stage == \"no-approval\":\n        print(f\"attavo drive: refused: approval is {extra}\")\n        return 1, True\n\n    branch = git(root, \"rev-parse\", \"--abbrev-ref\", \"HEAD\").strip()\n\n    if stage == \"build\":\n        return _drive_run_stage(root, records_dir, branch, \"build\", item_id)\n\n    checks_command = (m.get(\"forge\") or {}).get(\"checks_command\")\n    if not checks_command:\n        print(\"attavo drive: refused: no [forge].checks_command in the manifest\")\n        return 1, True\n    head_sha = git(root, \"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\").strip()\n    forge_checks, ok = poll_forge_checks(root, checks_command, head_sha)\n    if not ok:\n        conclusions = (\", \".join(f\"{c['name']} {c['conclusion']}\"\n                                 for c in forge_checks[\"conclusions\"])\n                      or \"no checks reported\")\n        print(f\"attavo drive: stopped: forge checks not green on {head_sha[:12]}: {conclusions}\")\n        return 1, True\n\n    if stage == \"verify\":\n        return _drive_run_stage(root, records_dir, branch, \"verify\", item_id)\n\n    print(f\"attavo drive: item {item_id} is ready to merge\")\n    return 0, True\n\n\ndef cmd_drive(argv: list[str]) -> int:\n    # Issue #145: Python block-buffers stdout when it is not a tty (a\n    # pipe, e.g. a screen reading this process live), so every stage\n    # line -- attavo build:, attavo drive: pushed, attavo verify:, attavo\n    # drive: item N is ready to merge -- would otherwise sit unflushed\n    # until this process exits. Line-buffering makes a pipe reader see\n    # each line the moment it is printed, the same as a terminal already\n\nThe file is never a record and is never committed: it is not among the\npaths `commit_record` names, and a tenant's `.gitignore` is not touched\nfor it, since an uncommitted file in an item's own worktree is already\ninvisible to the ledger and the doctor. An adapter that never writes to\nit breaks nothing -- the file is simply absent, and the screen renders\nthe feed it has without one.\n\n## Counsel (issue #100)\n\nWith `ATTAVO_STAGE=counsel`, the report line carries `class`, `verdict`,\n`citations`, and `proposal` instead of `status`/`question`:\n\n```json\n{\"class\": \"<stop class, or \\\"unclassed\\\">\",\n \"verdict\": \"answer\" | \"patch\" | \"person\",\n \"citations\": [{\"kind\": \"instruction\" | \"mechanism\" | \"guard\",\n                \"path\": \"<path>\" | null, \"symbol\": \"<symbol>\" | null,\n                \"ruling_id\": \"<id>\" | null}, ...],\n \"proposal\": {\"ruling_text\": \"<text>\" | null,\n              \"patch\": {\"file\": \"<path>\", \"symbol\": \"<symbol>\",\n                        \"rejection_test\": \"<test>\", \"diff\": \"<unified diff>\"} | null,\n              \"question\": \"<one line>\" | null},\n \"model\": \"<id>\", \"model_version\": \"<id or null>\",\n \"adapter_version\": \"<tool version>\",\n \"tokens\": {\"input\": n, \"output\": n}, \"cost_usd\": n | null}\n```\n\nCounsel is read-only -- the same sandbox verify runs under -- and it\nnever acts: `proposal` carries a ruling's text or a patch's diff, never\na write or a commit. `adapters/<name>/conformance.json` declares which\nstages an adapter supports as `\"stages\"`; an adapter naming no\n`\"counsel\"` there has not been pointed at this stage.\n\n## Two stops (issue #88)\n\nA `\"question\"` is not always a question. A status report landing in the\nquestion channel (\"I've traced the spec's 5 criteria and dispatched an\nimplementation agent…\") halts an item to a person exactly like a real\nquestion does, but the person has nothing to rule on. Every adapter\ntherefore treats a `\"question\"` whose text clears the length gate but\ncarries no `\"?\"` as a report, not a question: it re-invokes the executor\nonce more, in the same invocation, appending \"your last answer was a\nreport, not a question; either continue to done, or ask one question\nending in a question mark that names the spec line or guard you cannot\nresolve\" to the same instruction. A proper question or a `\"done\"` on the\nretry ends the invocation normally, at the cost of one extra executor\nturn; a second report in a row still halts (someone must look), but its\nreport line carries `\"class\": \"non-question\"` and both raw results\nalready reached the transcript above it. No record field changes: the\nclass lives in the transcript and the report line, not in\n`build-NN.json`.\n\nThe report line's own `tokens` and `cost_usd` sum both calls, whatever\nthe retry's outcome — the record's cost matches the two executor turns\nactually spent, not just the second one; only `model`/`model_version`/\n`adapter_version` come from the second call.\n\nWork can also outlive the invocation that started it: a tool that spawns\nits own background subagents can report `\"done\"` and exit while those\nsubagents are still running or still being torn down, so the checks\n`attavo build` runs right after `\"done\"` land on a tree something else\nis still writing. An adapter whose tool can do this must refuse the run\nby name -- exit 1, with the counts -- rather than report `\"done\"` on it;\n`adapters/<name>/conformance.json` names this ability as\n`\"subagent_guard\": {\"supported\": true, \"situation\": \"<name>\"}` or, when\n1:diff --git a/.attavo/bin/forge-attestation b/.attavo/bin/forge-attestation\n32:diff --git a/.github/workflows/ci.yml b/.github/workflows/ci.yml\n79:diff --git a/attavo.toml b/attavo.toml\n8387:diff --git a/bin/attavo b/bin/attavo\n8522:diff --git a/schema/manifest.schema.json b/schema/manifest.schema.json\n8543:diff --git a/tests/test_doctor.py b/tests/test_doctor.py\n8572:diff --git a/tests/test_doctor_sealed.py b/tests/test_doctor_sealed.py\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
